Article 1: A party may raise a limitation of action defense against a claim for credit rights, but if a limitation of action defense is raised against any of the following claims for credit rights, the People's Court shall not support it:
(1) the claim for payment of principal and interest of deposits;
(2) the claim for redemption of principal and interest of government bonds, financial bonds, and corporate bonds issued to unspecified objects;
(3) the claim for contribution of capital arising from an investment relationship;
(4) other claims for credit rights to which the provisions on limitation of action are not applicable according to law.

Article 8: Where any of the following circumstances exists, it shall be determined that the "right holder's demand for performance made to the obligor" as provided in Article 195 of the Civil Code has occurred, and the effect of interruption of limitation of action shall be produced:
(1) one party directly delivers to the other party a document asserting rights, and the other party signs, seals, or affixes a fingerprint on the document, or although it does not sign, seal, or affix a fingerprint, it can be proved by other means that the document has reached the other party;
(2) one party asserts its rights by sending a letter or data message, and the letter or data message reaches or should reach the other party;
(3) one party is a financial institution, and deducts the principal and interest of the debt from the account of the other party in accordance with legal provisions or the agreement of the parties;
(4) one party is not traceable, and the other party publishes an announcement containing the content of asserting rights in a nationally influential media or in a provincial influential media at the place of domicile of the untraceable party, except that where laws and judicial interpretations otherwise provide special provisions, those provisions shall apply.
In the circumstances under item (1) of the preceding paragraph, if the other party is a legal person or other organization, the recipient may be its legal representative, principal responsible person, the department responsible for receiving and dispatching correspondence, or an authorized subject; if the other party is a natural person, the recipient may be the natural person himself, a family member living with him who has full capacity for civil conduct, or an authorized subject.

Article 11: The People's Court shall determine that any of the following matters has the same effect of interruption of limitation of action as filing a prosecution:
(1) applying for a payment order;
(2) applying for bankruptcy or declaring bankruptcy claims;
(3) applying for declaration of the obligor as missing or dead for the purpose of asserting rights;
(4) applying for pre-litigation measures such as pre-litigation property preservation and pre-litigation temporary injunction;
(5) applying for compulsory execution;
(6) applying for adding a party or being notified to participate in litigation;
(7) asserting set-off in litigation;
(8) other matters that have the same effect of interruption of limitation of action as filing a prosecution.
